##### CHAPTER II

##### PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE

## Part One

Principles Article 2 The Principles of Women's Empowerment and Protection are: a. protection;  
b. non-discrimination;  
c. kinship;  
d. inclusiveness;  
e. synergy;  
f. order and legal certainty;  
g. sustainability; and h. accountability.

## Part Two

Purpose Article 3 This Regional Regulation is established to serve as a guideline for the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Women's Protection by the Regional Government, business sector and the Community in development services in the Region.

## Part Three

Objectives Article 4 The objectives of establishing this Regional Regulation are as follows:
a. to serve as a guide for the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Women's Protection by the Regional Government, business sector and the Community;  
b. to guarantee legal certainty in the implementation of Women's Empowerment and Protection by the Regional Government, business sector and the Community;  
c. to realize synergy between the Regional Government, business sector and the Community in providing effective, efficient, transparent and accountable services for handling Women's Empowerment and Protection for victims of violence;  
d. to empower Women both individually, in families, groups and the Community so that they are able to live independently; and e. to prevent, serve and handle women victims of violence and women experiencing vulnerability to achieve independence.  
6-

## Part Four Scope

Article 5 The scope of this Regional Regulation is as follows:
a. women's rights;  
b. Women's Empowerment;  
c. Women's Protection;  
d. guidance and supervision; and e. Community participation.

### CHAPTER III

### WOMEN'S RIGHTS

Article 6 Every Woman in the Region has the right:
a. to life;  
b. to form a family and continue offspring; c. to self-development;  
d. to obtain justice;  
e. to security;  
f. to welfare; and g. to participate in government.  

Article 7 (1) The right to life as referred to in Article 6 letter a, includes:
a. the right to sustain life and improve living standards;  
b. the right to obtain tranquility, security, peace, happiness, physical and spiritual welfare; and c. the right to a good and healthy environment. (2) The right to form a family and continue offspring as referred to in Article 6 letter b, namely to form a family, continue offspring through lawful marriage in accordance with religion and laws and regulations.  
(3) The right to self-development as referred to in Article 6 letter c, includes: a. the right to fulfillment of basic needs to grow and develop properly;  
b. the right to protection for personal development, to obtain education, educate oneself and improve quality of life to become a faithful, pious, responsible, noble, happy, and prosperous human being in accordance with human rights;  
c. the right to develop and obtain benefits from science and technology, arts and culture for the welfare of oneself, nation and state;  
7-
d. the right to obtain information to develop oneself and social environment in accordance with religious, social and legal norms;  
e. the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available means in accordance with religious, social and legal norms;  
f. the right to strive for self-development, both personally and collectively, to build society, nation, and state in accordance with religious, social and legal norms;  
g. the right to participate in conducting social activities in accordance with religious, social and legal norms; h. the right to participate in planning and implementing government policies in accordance with laws and regulations;  
i. the right to assemble and associate in accordance with laws and regulations; and j. the right to obtain education and teaching in accordance with religious, social and legal norms.  
(4) The right to obtain justice as referred to in Article 6 letter d, includes: a. the right to receive equal treatment without discrimination; and b. the right to submit applications, complaints, and lawsuits, whether in criminal, civil, or administrative cases;  
(5) The right to security as referred to in Article 6 letter e, includes: a. the right to protection of oneself, family, honor, dignity, and property rights;  
b. the right to protection from violence in digital space including cyber harassment;  
c. the right to recognition before the law as a human person; and d. the right to be free from threats that violate religious, social and legal norms.  
(6) The right to welfare as referred to in Article 6 letter f, includes: a. the right to perform work and receive fair wages in accordance with laws and regulations;  
b. the right to maternity leave, granted to working Mothers for at least the first 3 (three) months, and the next 3 (three) months if there are special conditions proven by a doctor's certificate;  
c. the right to obtain rest time, granted to working Mothers for 1.5 (one and a half) months or in accordance with a doctor's certificate, or midwife in case of miscarriage;  
8-
d. the right to obtain opportunities and proper facilities for health and nutrition services and to perform lactation during working hours;  
e. the right to guarantee the fulfillment of the Mother's rights, the husband has the right to obtain leave to accompany his wife during childbirth, for 2 (two) days and may be granted for a maximum of the next 3 (three) days or as agreed.  
(7) The right to participate in government as referred to in Article 6 letter g, includes: a. the right to be elected and to vote;  
b. the right to pursue a career in government; and c. the right to convey aspirations directly or through intermediaries in government.

### CHAPTER IV

### WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT

## Part One

Implementation of Women's Empowerment Article 8 (1) The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility in efforts for Women's Empowerment. (2) Women's Empowerment efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are carried out in an integrated manner in accordance with laws and regulations. (3) Women's Empowerment as referred to in paragraph (1), includes:
a. socio-cultural;  
b. economic;  
c. legal; and d. political.  

Article 9 (1) Women's Empowerment in the socio-cultural field as referred to in Article 8 paragraph (3) letter a is implemented by Regional Apparatus responsible for Women's Empowerment, social, cultural, education, health, tourism affairs with the participation of individuals, the Community, the education sector and business sector.  

(2) Women's Empowerment in the socio-cultural field as referred to in paragraph (1) is implemented through: a. increasing knowledge, attitudes and skills to encourage the fulfillment of formal education in stages and strengthening women's capacity in informal and non-formal education according to potential to improve their social status;  
9-
b. increasing awareness, willingness and ability to overcome health problems through quality promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts;  
c. strengthening women's capacity through social institutions and/or cultural preservation center institutions;  
d. increasing awareness and knowledge about independent, healthy and prosperous family planning including access to consultation services and marriage registration;  
e. active participation of women in socializing and educating the Community to develop themselves in the social and cultural fields;  
f. involving women in various socio-cultural and tourism events at various levels both local, national and international; and g. facilitation and efforts to preserve customs and develop science, technology, sports, arts and culture for the advancement of Women.

